add('K64R2ReviewToolV2', 'reviewer', `Code review of the integrated tool ${TOOL} (contract kumitate-report-receipt-diff-v2, committed at ${INT}; wave 1 first built v1 at ${FREEZE}).
Read it fully. It merges K64ExecReceiptForge's forgery fixes with ports from K64ExecReceiptRealRun and K64ReviewTool. Look for: regexes that mis-handle the new normalisation scan() (collapsing ' = ' and ' / ' inside prose can CREATE a tally, e.g. 'X_HELD held = 3 / 3 of the seats' or a path 'a / b'), unfence() on unbalanced fences, CRLF reports, '~~~' fences, tabs; TALLY case-insensitivity colliding with ordinary words; the cited-line scoping (a claim line citing receipts only accepts those; what if a wrapped line cites the receipt on the next line?); the glob matcher globRe and directory-citation arm (a '*' alone, '**'); HASH regex {39,40} false positives on 39-hex substrings of sha256 digests (the report quotes sha256 values! a 64-hex digest contains 40-hex substrings - word-boundary behaviour?); inUrl() dynamic RegExp built from report text (injection/ReDoS); the --opt=value flatMap (a value containing '='); exit codes. For each defect: severity, reproducing command (fixture lane under /tmp/kumitate-r64/K64R2ReviewToolV2/), minimal diff. Do not edit the tool.`, ['operability'], 'review tool v2');

add('K64R2ReviewToolGit', 'reviewer', `Review ${TOOL} for git-mode versus worktree-mode equivalence and trust-boundary problems.
The wave-1 reviewer found receipts read via 'git ls-tree -r' + 'git show' in rev mode but readdirSync in worktree mode; v2 changed both (ls-tree -z, lstat). Build fixtures (D=$(mktemp -d) && git -C "$D" init -q; every git call -C "$D"; commit only inside it with -c user.name=fixture -c user.email=fixture@example.invalid) and show, for each of: nested receipt dirs, a receipt with a space/newline/non-ASCII name, a 70 MB receipt, a symlinked receipt, a gitlink (submodule) path, a receipt that is deleted in the worktree but committed, a receipt that is untracked but present in the worktree, a rev given as a tag/branch/HEAD~1/abbreviated hash, a shallow clone, --fleet pointing at a non-repo, whether worktree mode and rev mode print the same verdict. Also: the tool reads --report from disk, not git: a report edited after the cited rev (the L1 files the report after the lane tip!) — is anything bound to the report bytes? State what a non-author needs to pin (report blob hash) for the diff to be reproducible. Findings with reproducers and minimal diffs; do not edit the tool.`, ['operability'], 'review tool git semantics');

add('K64R2ReviewEntryTable', 'reviewer', `Attack the C2 entry's decision table and claims: ${ENTRY}.
For EVERY row of the '## Decision table' and every sentence of '## What the demo measures' and '## Mechanism': find the demo key (${DEMO}; ${ROUTE.demo}) or the located quote that supports it, run the demo yourself, and judge: SUPPORTED / OVER-CLAIMED (says more than the key shows) / UNSUPPORTED / WRONG. Pay particular attention to: (a) 'two seats abandon each other's empty heads ... both end fresh' and the hand-measured clause about content on both sides (check ${OUT('K64ExecJjDemoHarden')}/measurements-abandon-matrix.txt actually contains it); (b) the guard column — is each guard actually sufficient or merely plausible (e.g. 'commit or copy files out before update-stale': does a committed-but-not-checked-out change survive? test in a fixture); (c) the row 'a seat must not see another seat's claims — impossible inside one repository': is that proven or inferred; (d) the version-skew paragraph. Reproduce disagreeing facts in a fresh mktemp fixture (explicit -R/-C, jj --config user.name=fixture --config user.email=fixture@example.invalid, JJ_CONFIG=/dev/null). Output a table: sentence/row, verdict, evidence, proposed correction text.`, ['depth', 'calibration'], 'attack entry table');

add('K64R2ReviewEntryLimits', 'reviewer', `Attack the entry's central rule and its limits with NEW experiments the demo does not run.
The entry says jj re-points a seat's working copy on its own when the files on disk already equal the new commit's tree and marks it stale otherwise ([INFERENCE] fit to four outcomes, ${ENTRY}). Design and run >= 8 further fixture experiments (fresh mktemp fixtures, with jj --config user.name=fixture --config user.email=fixture@example.invalid, explicit -R, JJ_CONFIG=/dev/null; never the lane) that could falsify the rule: a rewrite that changes the tree below the seat's @ (rebase onto a different parent), a rebase of the seat's commit by the default workspace, a squash FROM the seat's commit, 'jj restore', 'jj edit' of the seat's commit from the default workspace (two workspaces on one commit), an untracked ignored file in the seat, a seat with a conflict, a seat whose working copy holds a pending (unsnapshotted) edit at the moment of the rewrite, an abandon of the seat's PARENT. Record each outcome, say which fit the rule and which break it, and propose the corrected rule text and any extra demo keys (as a diff to scripts/jj-seat-demo.sh in your build directory ${OUT('K64R2ReviewEntryLimits')}/). Also check every item in '## Limits' is true and that no unmeasured claim is unlabelled.`, ['depth', 'coverage'], 'falsify stale rule');

add('K64R2ReviewRows', 'reviewer', `Attack registry rows S198, S199, S200 (${LANE}/corpus/SOURCES.md) against CORPUS.md SOURCES contract v1 (/home/vgpnk/Projects/shokunin/CORPUS.md) and the lane's QUALITY.md.
Cell by cell: kind, tier (is A defensible for each scope? Firefox docs = a deployment's contributor documentation; S200 = a second rendering of a page S067 already pins), scope, anchor (section + revision; do the section names exist verbatim in the captures ${CAP}/*), teaches (a grounded learner benefit, each phrase supported by the capture), status/opened/recheck/used-by, no '|' inside a cell, ids unused. Then: the round-64 row notes inserted after the table (are they inside ## Registry and harmless to the parsers?), the duplicate-URL policy (source-dupes gate, W04) and the S200-vs-S067 same-document question (CORPUS rule 4: a second row about one document), capture pins in .ufo/pins/source-captures.json (sha256 and bytes recomputed by you), and the staged rows' originals in ${ROUTE.staged}/SOURCES.append.md (S259/S270/S290/S291): did the lane row drop or alter a fact? Reproduce every number with a command. Defects with minimal diffs; do not edit corpus/.`, ['coverage', 'calibration'], 'attack registry rows');

add('K64R2ReviewLesson', 'reviewer', `Attack the round lesson ${LANE}/corpus/lessons/round-64.md line by line (K63R2ReviewLesson found seven stale or false statements in the previous round's; do at least as well).
For every sentence containing a number, a path, a hash, a command or a verdict word: run the command / open the path / git cat-file the hash and mark TRUE / FALSE / STALE / UNVERIFIABLE. Specifically check: the §Mistakes rows (class names from the CORPUS.md vocabulary: reference-drift is listed, 'unreceipted-tally' is not — is a new class permitted?), whether each fix 'was shown able to fail' with a committed red-before receipt, the §What round 64 changes bullets (the Q60 epoch sentence, selftest 52/52, 37 controls, 21 keys), the §Gate table (every quote re-found in a live fetch by YOU, access date, word counts <= 25, the S198/S200 URLs now v0.46.0), the collision-guard numbers, the carried rejects, the considerthese quotes (open ${LANE}/considerthese/2026-10-25.md and compare verbatim). Also: does the file avoid the placeholder/template classes the new tool flags (run node ${TOOL} on a copy of it as a document with --round 64)? Output a table of every defect with file:line, evidence and exact replacement text.`, ['calibration', 'depth'], 'attack lesson');

add('K64R2ReviewQuality', 'reviewer', `Attack the criteria appended to ${LANE}/corpus/QUALITY.md: Q60, Q61 and Q51-C1 (committed at ${INT}).
For each: does it state who checks, a runnable command, a fails-when list and a shown-able-to-fail receipt, and does every quoted receipt line occur in the cited file? Run each command now and compare with the quoted output. Q60: epoch wording against scripts/plan-before-ready.sh (PBR_MAP_FROM, default 65) — run 'bash scripts/plan-before-ready.sh --round 64', 'PBR_MAP_FROM=0 bash scripts/plan-before-ready.sh --round 64', '--round 63', '--all' and 'bash scripts/plan-before-ready.sh --selftest' and compare. Q61: finding ids vs the tool's real ids, counts (37 controls, 52 selftest) vs reality, the round-60/63 real-data claims. Q51-C1: re-run the hex-token sweep (grep -oE '\\b[0-9a-f]{7,40}\\b' corpus/QUALITY.md | sort -u) and confirm which tokens resolve in the lane or the fleet (git cat-file -e h^{commit}); confirm that 923a172 resolves and is the commit the r49 spot-check names. Check that each criterion grounds in a lesson that exists (corpus/lessons/round-64.md §Mistakes) and that numbering continues Q59 with no gap. Output defects with line anchors and corrected text.`, ['calibration'], 'attack QUALITY rows');

add('K64R2ReviewPbr', 'reviewer', `Code review of the readme-map arm added to ${LANE}/scripts/plan-before-ready.sh (integrated at ${INT}; K64ExecReadmeGuard's draft plus the L1's epoch variable MAP_FROM=PBR_MAP_FROM default 65, selftest sets 0).
Read the whole script. Look for: the epoch comparison ('[ "$round" -lt "$MAP_FROM" ] 2>/dev/null' — a non-numeric round or PBR_MAP_FROM silently skips the arm?); the archive extraction (tar --no-same-owner, path traversal in a hostile archive, temp dir cleanup on signals); node being resolved from PATH inside the archive (the archived readme-map.mjs is executed with the operator's privileges: a plan commit carrying a malicious scripts/readme-map.mjs runs on every gate invocation — judge and say what bounds it); MAP_ABSENT logic (git log --diff-filter=A across history); 'timeout' fallback; verdict-line grammar changes breaking existing consumers (grep for callers of plan-before-ready output: scripts/*.mjs, QUALITY.md, SKILL.md: does anything parse the old line shape?); --all output and summary 'map_red='. Reproduce each defect with a command in fixtures built as D=$(mktemp -d) && git -C "$D" init -q (explicit -C always). Minimal diffs; do not edit the script.`, ['operability'], 'review pbr arm');

add('K64R2ReviewDrafts', 'reviewer', `Attack the wave-1 scribe drafts that the L1 will integrate at close (they are NOT installed yet): ${OUT('K64ScribeScorecard')}/scorecard-r64.md, ${OUT('K64ScribeLogSkill')}/{log-entry.md,skill-rows.md,checklist-item.md}, ${OUT('K64ScribeErratum')}/erratum.md.
For each file: every number, path:line anchor, insertion-point line number (they were computed at the freeze commit; ${LANE}/SCORECARD.md, SKILL.md and LOG.md have not changed since except the PROVINCE/README/QUALITY/SOURCES integration — recompute every anchor against the current files), every contract name (report-receipt-diff is now v2, jj-seat-demo v2, 52-case selftest, 37 controls, 21 demo keys, 3 new capability facts), every claim about what the tools do. List each stale or false item with the corrected text, and flag any statement that pre-claims a measured result (the draft uses @@TOKEN@@ placeholders for unmeasured values — verify none was filled with an unrun number). Also check the SKILL.md gate-registry row format against the existing rows (grep -n 'inline-dispatch-evidence' SKILL.md) and whether scripts/claims-check.mjs or proxy-check.mjs parse those rows (read them; say what breaks if the row shape is wrong).`, ['calibration', 'depth'], 'attack integration drafts');

add('K64R2CriticForgeV2', 'critic', `Forge the integrated receipt tool v2 (${TOOL}). Wave 1's forge seat found 20 lies; find the next 12.
Start from K64ConnForgeryClasses' ten open forgeries (${LANE}/.ufo/waves/round-64/w1/K64ConnForgeryClasses.md), the 20 cases already adopted (${CTL}/k*), and K64ExecReceiptForge's inherent-limits list (${LANE}/.ufo/waves/round-64/w1/K64ExecReceiptForge.md). New ideas to try: a labelled tally whose label contains lower-case or digits-first characters; 'HELD' spelled with Unicode confusables; a tally in an HTML comment; a tally split across two lines ('X9_HELD\\nheld=3/3'); a report that cites the receipt by a different but resolving path (case-folding, './' prefix, double slash); numbers with leading zeros (03/03) or full-width digits; an honest-looking receipt added to close/ in the same round as a late forgery (the 'trusted as written' limit: show the cheapest in-lane forgery of a receipt that makes a false claim CLEAN and say what binds a receipt to a counter run); hashes that are valid commits of ANOTHER lane repo under the fleet root; a hash present in the fleet repo only. Build each as a control in the frozen format (report.md, lane/, expect) under ${OUT('K64R2CriticForgeV2')}/cases/ with the verdict a sound tool must give, run the tool, and report which read CLEAN. For each killable one: the minimal patch (unified diff on a scratch copy; run the 37 controls and selftest to prove no regression).`, ['operability', 'calibration'], 'forge tool v2');

add('K64R2CriticForgeStruct', 'critic', `Forge the report STRUCTURE checks of ${TOOL} and the P5 arm of ${ROUTE.c1probe}.
The tool requires six headings and a SHONEN section with >= 4 box lines. The round-64 report must satisfy them; the question is what else a lying or lazy report can do while reading CLEAN: headings present with empty bodies; the six headings in the wrong order; a SHONEN block of four '[ ]' boxes claiming nothing; boxes marked [x] with no named verifier (PROTOCOL.md: only a non-author may write [x]) — read /home/vgpnk/Projects/shokunin/PROTOCOL.md for the report contract and list contract clauses the tool does not check but a closure would (commit-hash citation present, 'Sources & frontier' line 'SOURCES:' form, a Trend verdicts entry per trend, the L0 questions). Propose the cheapest additional arms (with names and the exact regexes) and prove with fixtures that each would have flagged a real round-N report from the fleet (git -C ${FLEET} show <commit>:.ufo/reports/round-N/kumitate.md for N in 40..63) without flagging round 63's. Do not write to scripts/; deliver patch text.`, ['operability'], 'forge structure checks');

add('K64R2CriticJjForge', 'critic', `Re-attack the C2 counter ${ROUTE.c2probe} at the integrated revision ${INT}, where the honest entry reads JJ64_HELD 8/8.
Wave 1 (K64CriticC2Predicate, ${LANE}/.ufo/waves/round-64/w1/K64CriticC2Predicate.md) built four forgeries that read 8/8 on the frozen counter. Reproduce them against the REAL integrated tree in a sandbox clone (git clone --no-hardlinks of the lane at ${INT} into mktemp; commit inside the clone only with -c user.name=fixture -c user.email=fixture@example.invalid; verify git -C "$C" rev-parse --show-toplevel): (1) the entry rewritten with different true-sounding but wrong demo-relevant prose while the Demo outcomes section stays set-equal; (2) an entry whose Located quotes are real but whose body claims are invented; (3) a demo script that echoes the entry's stated values (J6) — does the committed scripts/jj-seat-demo.sh at ${INT} resist a replaced demo (what binds the demo bytes? the pin registry? counter-pins?); (4) a changed capture + recomputed pin (J3 relies on the pin the lane writes). For each: reads HELD or UNMET, and the cheapest supplementary check outside the counter that would catch it (name it precisely; K64R2ExecSupplementaryC2 is writing the supplementary checker — message agent K64R2ExecSupplementaryC2 only if your finding changes its scope, one short line).`, ['calibration'], 'forge C2 counter on real tree');

add('K64R2MutateToolV2', 'mutation-tester', `Mutation battery on the integrated tool ${TOOL} (v2; 52 selftest cases) and the 37 controls ${CTL}.
Copy the tool to ${OUT('K64R2MutateToolV2')}/work/ (keep the work dir small: delete mutant copies after each run; the lane's proxy gate counts every .mjs under the lane tree). Write mutants.mjs applying ONE single-literal mutant at a time (>= 60: each regex in PLACEHOLDER/TALLY/RECEIPT_PATH/HASH, scan()'s three replacements, unfence's fence tests, the SHONEN level comparison, boxes threshold, the cited-scope filter, the checks= comparison, symlink mode 120000 skip, isPath '..' test, glob/dir arms, inUrl, tip fallback, the --opt= flatMap, each exit code and each finding id). Run each against (a) the selftest and (b) all 37 controls via the invocation of the frozen counter's P2 (${ROUTE.c1probe}: lane=<case>/lane, --round 9, --report <case>/report.md, no --rev). Report killed-by-selftest / killed-by-controls / SURVIVORS. For each survivor: equivalent (reasoning) or the minimal selftest case that kills it (patch text). Table: id, mutation, killed-by, verdict. K64ExecReceiptMutants did 70 against the v1 tool (33 survived; ${LANE}/.ufo/waves/round-64/w1/K64ExecReceiptMutants.md): say how many of ITS survivors v2 now kills.`, ['operability'], 'mutate tool v2');

add('K64R2MutateEntry', 'mutation-tester', `Mutation-test the C2 entry against the frozen counter ${ROUTE.c2probe} (JJ64) at ${INT}.
In a sandbox clone (git clone --no-hardlinks of the lane into mktemp, checkout ${INT}, commit mutants inside the clone only with -c user.name=fixture -c user.email=fixture@example.invalid; verify git -C "$C" rev-parse --show-toplevel) apply single mutants to ${ENTRY} and to its rows/pins and run 'node .ufo/waves/round-64/probe/jj64-probe.mjs "$C" <rev> --no-live' for each (and --live for 5 of them): flip each of the 21 Demo outcomes values; delete one; add an invented key; change a quote by one word; push a quote to 26 words; drop the recheck line; change verified-at; remove a used-by; change a pin sha; drop PROVINCE's entry path; edit agent-isolation-substrates.md in place (not append); delete the amendment heading; add a 22nd demo key live. >= 30 mutants. Table: mutation, arms that fire (J1..J8), UNMET/HELD. A mutant that stays HELD although the entry became false is a counter hole: list it with the cheapest fix and note that the counter is frozen (reported, not patched).`, ['calibration', 'coverage'], 'mutate entry/rows');

add('K64R2MutatePbr', 'mutation-tester', `Mutation battery on the plan-before-ready arm: ${LANE}/scripts/plan-before-ready.sh (selftest 18 cases, integrated at ${INT}).
Copy the script to ${OUT('K64R2MutatePbr')}/work/ and apply single-literal mutants (>= 30): the epoch comparison and its default 65, MAP_FROM=0 in run_selftest, each MAP_FAIL_REASON branch, the 'CLEAN' grep, exit-code tests (0/124/127), the first-add ancestry test, the timeout 300, --no-same-owner, the marker/plan ordering arms (the pre-existing Q58 logic must keep its own kills), the summary 'map_red' field. Run the selftest on each; report SURVIVORS and, for each, the minimal selftest case that kills it (a patch to the selftest text, as a diff in your build directory). Note which mutants die only because the selftest is brittle on output text rather than verdict.`, ['operability'], 'mutate pbr arm');

